DESCRIPTION

This test validates that starved SCHED_NORMAL tasks can be boosted with either
stalld or dl-server,

A high-priority hogger task (stress-ng at SCHED_FIFO(1)) is used to continuously
occupy the core. A starver task (SCHED_NORMAL) is pinned to the same core, which
would normally be starved.

The test passes if the starver task gets scheduled, which is verified by
observing its sched_switch events. This confirms that the kernel's starvation
relief mechanism (either stalld or dl-server) has successfully boosted the
starver, allowing it to preempt the hogger.


CONFIGURATION

* The hogger and starver processes are pined to an isolated core to minimize the
  impact on housekeeping.
* Hogger: stress-ng running a CPU-bound load in FIFO(1).
* Starver: A SCHED_NORMAL process that repeatedly checks and prints its own
  sched policy.
* Stalld: In our setting the period is 200ms, runtime is 1ms, and boost duration
  is 1s, process will get boosted when they get starved for 1s. Basically when a
  task is starved for 1s, then for the next 1s it will be SCHED_DEADLINE, and
  for every 200ms it gets 1ms runtime. That's the design anyways..


NOTES

The overall goal is to verify that starved, non-real-time threads are correctly
boosted. The specific mechanism for this has changed.

* Prior to 10.1: stalld must be explicitly run to detect and boost any starved
  task to SCHED_DEADLINE.
* 10.1+: The kernel's dl-server (fair-server) is enabled by default. It provides
  a runtime bandwidth to starved SCHED_NORMAL tasks. In this environment, stalld
  automatically enters a "log-only" mode and no longer performs boosts,
  deferring to dl-server.


KNOWN ISSUES

* ktimers/N threads run in FIFO(1) which is the same priority with the hogger
  process. Both the starver task and the timeout option in stress-ng command
  rely on the ktimers/N to handle the timer request, and that will essentially
  block our testing on el10 with the log-only stalld and dl-server combination
  as RT tasks won't get boosted. As a workaround the ktimers/N thread on the
  test core is manually changed to FIFO(5) and restored after testing.